# Copy this to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored (local/secret values).
#
# ONE database everywhere: PostgreSQL. Local dev and production run the SAME
# schema on the SAME engine — they differ only by this connection string
# (your machine's Postgres locally; a Neon Postgres URL in production).
# There is no SQLite and no second database anywhere.

# --- Database (PostgreSQL) ---
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres@127.0.0.1:5432/ecom_analytics?schema=public"
# In production, set this in the host's env vars to your Neon POOLED URL, e.g.:
# postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@ep-xxxx-pooler.REGION.aws.neon.tech/DB?sslmode=require

# --- Auth ---
# Secret that signs session cookies. Use a long random value in production.
AUTH_SECRET="dev-secret-change-me-in-production-0123456789abcdef"

# --- Live sync (production only) ---
# Where this deployment is reached from the internet. Used to register
# WooCommerce webhooks so order changes stream in the moment they happen.
# On Vercel this can be left unset — the production URL is picked up
# automatically. Leave unset locally: a webhook can't reach localhost.
# APP_URL="https://analytics.example.com"

# Bearer token Vercel Cron sends when calling the scheduled sync.
# Without it the scheduled sync refuses to run. Set it in the host's env vars.
# CRON_SECRET="a-long-random-value"

# Shared secret Postmark's inbound webhook must present as ?token=... when it
# POSTs the warehouse's daily report to /api/delivery/inbound. Without it the
# route refuses every request. Set it in the host's env vars, and configure
# the identical value on the inbound webhook's URL in Postmark's dashboard.
# DELIVERY_INBOUND_SECRET="a-long-random-value"

# --- Outbound email ---
# What sends the "Forgot password" link. The SAME Postmark account that receives
# the warehouse's daily report, so there is one vendor and one bill — but this
# is the SERVER API TOKEN for the outbound stream, a different value from
# DELIVERY_INBOUND_SECRET above (which is our own shared secret, not Postmark's).
#
# EMAIL_FROM must be an address Postmark has verified as a Sender Signature, or
# on a domain verified there. Postmark refuses anything else with "Sender
# signature not confirmed", and while the account is in test mode it will only
# deliver to addresses on the account itself.
#
# Leave both unset and nothing breaks: the forgot-password route logs the
# failure and still answers exactly as it does on success, so the page can never
# be used to discover which addresses have a login. The link simply never
# arrives, which is why these belong in the host's env vars in production.
# POSTMARK_SERVER_TOKEN="..."
# EMAIL_FROM="no-reply@yourdomain.com"

# --- Ad platform apps ---
# The Meta app and Google OAuth client behind "Connect with Facebook" and
# "Connect with Google". These used to be a form the client filled in, which
# made him register a Facebook app and apply for a Google developer token
# before either button worked. They are server config: set them here and he
# presses one button and picks his ad accounts.
#
# Paste the PLAINTEXT value straight from the platform's own dashboard: the
# Meta app's App ID and App Secret, the Google OAuth client's ID and secret,
# the Google Ads developer token from the API Center. A value copied straight
# out of the AdPlatformApp database row is also accepted, for compatibility —
# but that value is enc:v1: ciphertext tied to whichever AUTH_SECRET
# encrypted it, so it breaks if pasted where a different AUTH_SECRET is in
# effect, and breaks here too the day AUTH_SECRET is rotated. Plaintext has
# neither problem, so it is the one to use.
#
# Leave them unset and the app falls back to the AdPlatformApp row already in
# the database, so a typo here cannot take a working deployment down.
# META_APP_ID="..."
# META_APP_SECRET="..."
# GOOGLE_ADS_CLIENT_ID="....apps.googleusercontent.com"
# GOOGLE_ADS_CLIENT_SECRET="..."
# GOOGLE_ADS_DEVELOPER_TOKEN="..."

# --- AI advisor ---
# The morning briefing and the advisor chat. Without it, the Advisor page says
# so plainly and shows the computed facts on their own rather than erroring.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY="sk-ant-..."

# --- DHL parcel tracking ---
# The Consumer Key of a DHL developer-portal app subscribed to "Shipment
# Tracking - Unified". The API Secret issued alongside it is NOT used by this
# product and is deliberately not stored anywhere.
#
# Unlike Bring's credentials — which live in the DeliveryConfig row because the
# warehouse settings page edits them — this is server config, like every other
# API key in this file.
#
# Leave it unset and nothing breaks: DHL parcels keep the milestones the
# warehouse file gives them, and delivery/sync.ts skips them without recording
# an error, so connecting it later picks them up exactly where they were.
#
# MIND THE RATE LIMIT. The free tier is 250 calls a day at one call every five
# seconds, and DHL takes ONE tracking number per request — there is no batch
# form. delivery/sync.ts therefore spends at most DHL_CALLS_PER_RUN (2) per
# fifteen-minute run, which is 192 a day. That sustains roughly eighty open
# parcels at two looks each per day. Beyond that, ask DHL to raise the limit
# rather than raising the constant.
# DHL_API_KEY="..."
